    "summary": "Strong first set with Sugaree & Let It Grow; second half uneven",
    "blurb": "First set rolls through Hell In A Bucket, Sugaree and West L.A. Fadeaway with tight energy. Highlights include Let It Grow, the Iko jam and a lively Throwing Stones → Lovelight transition. The second set loses momentum toward the encore.",
    "review": "The Irvine Meadows show on April 18‑1987 opens with a punchy Hell In A Bucket that launches into a bright first set packed with solid choices—Sugaree, West L.A. Fadeaway and a soaring Let It Grow that showcases Jerry’s fluid lead work. Brent’s keyboards sit comfortably in the mix while the rhythm section drives the band forward. The second set kicks off with an exuberant Iko Iko jam and rolls into Playing In The Band and Terrapin Station before drifting into a more pedestrian run‑of‑the‑mill stretch; the highlight here is the playful Throwing Stones → Turn On Your Lovelight segue that briefly lifts the energy. By the encore, Black Muddy River feels like a drag, and Jerry’s vocal tone shows signs of wear. Overall, the show delivers strong moments—especially in the first set—but the second half lacks the narrative spark found on classic outings.\n\nAmong the available sources, the matrix recording gd1987-04-18.sbd.walker-scotton.miller.83732.sbeok.flac16 offers the clearest picture: a well‑balanced 50/50 mix of board and audience that captures the band’s dynamics without major dropouts. While not flawless, it presents the best listening experience for fans wanting to hear the standout performances of Sugaree, Let It Grow, Iko Iko and the Throwing Stones/Lovelight transition.",
